    Open the PR early so CI and bots run alongside the work. The code is ready only after the slop-strip per **Comments** and **unslop** and the `/no-comments` pass, so the full swarm runs on finished code. Then rebase onto current trunk and report the code-ready head before self-proof, so verification runs on the base the patch lands on. Report every later head, and merge-ready once self-proof, CI, and babysit are done. Fix rounds stay on that base unless trunk conflicts, breaks CI, or lands a known concurrent change to the same files, because each rebase restarts CI. Run the repo's checks for the touched paths on the committed head before the code-ready push and before a later push that needs a verifier.
 
-   Assume the agent stops. Soon after starting, push and start a decision trail per the **show-me-your-work** skill, then push each finished unit before you start the next. Whoever spawns a subagent records its id, state, and a stuck-after runtime from past runs of its kind in the task notes, so the root can probe it if the parent stops. Force-push only your own branch, with `--force-with-lease` pinned to the SHA you last pushed or took over, and on any push you did not make, stop and report to the root.
+   Assume the agent stops. Soon after starting, push and start a decision trail per the **show-me-your-work** skill, then push each finished unit before you start the next. Whoever starts a delegated run records its id, state, and a stuck-after runtime based on past runs of its kind in the owning lane's Botlake task, so the root can inspect it if the parent stops. Force-push only your own branch, with `--force-with-lease` pinned to the SHA you last pushed or took over, and on any push you did not make, stop and report to the root.
 3. **Run owners in parallel off trunk, never stacked,** so each PR lands on its own.
 4. **Swarm-verify once.** A full swarm runs once, at the code-ready head. Another full swarm runs only for new behavior, a redesign, or a large new diff. A rebase, a merge-conflict resolution, or a minor fix gets a single verifier at the new head, and a wording-only change gets none. Fan out independent verifiers at the code-ready SHA per the **swarm** skill. They re-run the gates, prove the load-bearing behavior live on the real surface through its project verification skill or a named driver, and audit the diff. The verdict is not clean without the live lane, or before you audit the merge-ready report's receipts, because no lane trusts a claim it has not checked.
 
@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@ A verdict covers one patch and green CI covers one head. Step 4 runs a full swar
    Right before merging, fetch trunk and ask whether its changes can affect the patch, because rebasing for every trunk move never ends. Rebase and wait for CI when the code that would land was never tested, as with a conflict, a file both sides changed, or a change to which checks run for the PR. A lockfile both sides changed needs no rebase when a frozen install passes on the merge result and the packages the PR changes resolve there as on the head, because that install tests the lockfile that would land, and a failing install means the rebase must repair it. When trunk changed a caller of the patch or something the patch depends on, such as code it calls or a dependency version, run the patch's checks on the merge result and merge on a pass. Otherwise merge. If trunk would force a third rebase past merge prep, hold and report to the root, which sequences the colliding work.
 6. **Run the root layer.** Anything that loosens a gate, such as a raised budget or an allowlist entry, needs a verifier's proof and then your fresh countersign, because the owner is the one who wants green. Under a grant that covers approvals, that countersign is the approval, and the owner's record of it points at your countersign for a lane to check. A disabled or soft-failed check always comes out, because it stops guarding every case. Never give or bypass an approval the forge enforces, because no countersign covers it.
 
-   On the operator's go, begin an hourly audit observation cycle until no delegated work is left, because long runs drift and agents stall silently. Use the Autonomous run wake mechanism; active waits do not persist after the coordinator stops. Each tick re-reads this playbook and fixes drift, such as an unreported head or an unrecorded agent, by having its owner record it, because drift is a gap in the record, not a stall. It probes every agent, down to the leaf lanes, and judges each by its side effects, because status text and summaries can hide a stuck child. An agent is stuck when it errors, cannot start turns, or shows nothing new past its stuck-after runtime, and so is an owner that has neither pushed nor written a trail row by the first tick. Check outstanding tools and branch activity; a wait timeout alone is not that evidence. One waiting on you or the operator is not stuck, so answer it. Replace a stuck agent promptly, with only one writer on its branch at a time. A tick that changed something for the operator ends by catching them up per the **ketchup** skill, and one that changed nothing stays quiet.
+   On the operator's go, begin an hourly audit observation cycle until no delegated work is left. Use the Autonomous run wake mechanism and inspect Botlake agent activity. Each tick re-reads this playbook and fixes drift, such as an unreported head or an unrecorded run, by having its owner update the Botlake task for that lane. Probe every agent, down to the leaf lanes, and judge progress by side effects, not status text or summaries. An agent is stuck when it errors, cannot start turns, or shows nothing new past its recorded stuck-after runtime. An owner that has neither pushed nor written a trail row by the first tick is also stuck. Check outstanding tools and branch activity; a wait timeout alone is not that evidence. An agent waiting on you or the operator is not stuck, so answer it. Replace a stuck agent promptly, with only one writer on its branch at a time. A tick that changed something for the operator ends by catching them up per the **ketchup** skill, and one that changed nothing stays quiet.
 7. **Stand down at once on the operator's stop.** A hold reaches every owner as a zero-writes order. Each reports where it stands and keeps its brief until the operator releases it.
 
 **Reply:** catch the operator up per the **ketchup** skill, covering each PR's state and verdict, what merged, countersigns and why, open operator gates, and parked follow-ups, with links to each PR and the trails.
